{%- doc -%}
  The four reassurances under the buy row (Figma: Standart Product Page → info).

  Unlike [_ui-product-chips] these carry no per-product override. The four are the
  shop's promises — gift-ready packaging, handcrafted, made to share — and they read
  the same on every treat, so they live on blocks in the theme editor and nowhere
  else. The two rows are separate because they are two components in the file and
  not one wearing a flag — these are a 24px glyph above
  its wording, spread across the column, where a tag is a 14px glyph inside an
  outlined pill.

  The row spreads rather than counting columns: each item keeps the width its
  wording needs and the slack goes between them, which is how the file draws four
  items of four different widths. Each has a floor, so once four of them no longer
  fit the column the row breaks to two rows of two — a phone needs no breakpoint
  here, and a merchant who adds a fifth item does not have to be given one.
{%- enddoc -%}

<div
  class="ui-product-assurances"
  style="
    --ui-product-assurances-gap: {{ block.settings.gap }}px;
    --ui-product-assurances-gap-mobile: {{ block.settings.gap_mobile }}px;
    --ui-product-assurances-min: {{ block.settings.min_width }}px;
  "
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {% content_for 'blocks' %}
</div>

{% stylesheet %}
  /*
   * Spread, not a grid of equal columns. The four items are drawn at four
   * different widths — 140, 77, 123 and 131 across the 586px column — and equal
   * columns cut the widest of them to 131 and wrapped "Gift-Ready Packaging" onto
   * a second line. Space-between gives each its own width and puts the slack
   * between them, which is what the file does.
   */
  .ui-product-assurances {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--ui-product-assurances-gap, 20px);
  }

  /* The floor that makes the row wrap instead of squeezing: four of these no
     longer fit a phone's column, so it breaks to two rows of two on its own. */
  .ui-product-assurances > * {
    flex: 0 1 auto;
    min-inline-size: var(--ui-product-assurances-min, 120px);
  }

  /*
   * Two even columns once four no longer fit on a line.
   *
   * A container query rather than a media query, because the width this row gets
   * is a share of the details column and not of the viewport — the column is a
   * grid track above 990px and the whole page below it. Measured against the
   * viewport instead, a 750px breakpoint would have broken the row into two rows
   * of two at 749px, where the stacked column is still 717px wide and all four sit
   * happily on one line.
   *
   * 620 is where four stop fitting. The figure is measured, not derived — a
   * container query cannot read a custom property — which means it tracks the
   * wording, and the wording has already moved once. It read 560 for labels
   * measuring 136, 120, 120 and 125; the row now carries "Handmade in small
   * batches" at 175px, and the four measure 126, 133, 175 and 107, which with
   * three 20px gaps comes to 601.
   *
   * That left the row in the dead band this number exists to close: the details
   * column is 586px, too narrow for four and too wide to trip a 560px switch, so
   * it wrapped where flex wraps — three on a line and the fourth orphaned under
   * them, against the left edge. Re-measure this when the labels change, and give
   * it headroom: below the threshold the arrangement is settled and further
   * growth in the wording costs nothing, while above it every extra word is
   * another chance to land back in the band.
   *
   * `space-between` is right for the full row — it is what the file draws — and
   * wrong the moment the row wraps, because two items on a line get flung to
   * opposite edges with a hole between them. The grid puts each in an even half
   * instead, so the pairs line up under each other.
   */
  @container (max-width: 620px) {
    .ui-product-assurances {
      display: grid;
      /*
       * Even columns still, but sized by the wording rather than by the room. Half
       * of the container is not what the items need: in the product page's 586px
       * column the halves came out 287 apiece around wording measuring 126, 133,
       * 175 and 106, which left 169px of nothing down the middle and read as two
       * separate columns rather than one block of four.
       */
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      /*
       * Two equal columns, but measured from the widest item rather than from the
       * column the row happens to sit in — `fit-content` is what stops 1fr from
       * meaning "half of whatever is going". The block then centres as a whole
       * instead of being stretched to the edges.
       */
      width: fit-content;
      margin-inline: auto;
      /*
       * The gap arrives as an inline custom property from the block's setting, and
       * an inline style outranks anything a stylesheet can say about that property
       * — so redeclaring `--ui-product-assurances-gap` here would lose silently.
       * This sets the gap itself, reading the separate mobile figure.
       * See docs/section-settings.md §6.
       *
       * Split, because the two directions are no longer doing the same job. Down
       * the block the setting is the whole space between a label and the next
       * glyph. Across it, the columns now touch at their own width, so the figure
       * that separated even halves would leave the pairs all but joined.
       */
      row-gap: var(--ui-product-assurances-gap-mobile, 12px);
      column-gap: var(--ui-product-assurances-column-gap, 48px);
    }

    /* The floor is what made the row wrap; in the grid the column decides. */
    .ui-product-assurances > * {
      min-inline-size: 0;
    }
  }

  .ui-product-assurances:not(:has(*)) {
    display: none;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Reassurance row",
  "tag": null,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "_ui-product-assurance"
    }
  ],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap",
      "label": "t:settings.gap",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 48,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 20
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap_mobile",
      "label": "t:content.mobile_size",
      "info": "The space between items once the row has broken to two rows of two, which happens on a column narrower than 620px \u2014 the product page's own column included.",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 48,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 12
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "min_width",
      "label": "Narrowest item",
      "info": "Where the row drops to fewer columns.",
      "min": 80,
      "max": 220,
      "step": 10,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 120
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Reassurance row",
      "blocks": {
        "packaging": {
          "type": "_ui-product-assurance",
          "settings": { "icon": "gift", "label": "Gift-ready on arrival" }
        },
        "gluten_free": {
          "type": "_ui-product-assurance",
          "settings": { "icon": "wheat-slash", "label": "Certified Gluten-Free" }
        },
        "handcrafted": {
          "type": "_ui-product-assurance",
          "settings": { "icon": "cookie", "label": "Handmade in small batches" }
        },
        "share_love": {
          "type": "_ui-product-assurance",
          "settings": { "icon": "hand-holding-heart", "label": "Packed with care" }
        }
      },
      "block_order": ["packaging", "gluten_free", "handcrafted", "share_love"]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
